{
  "dom_id": "step-q163799",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14117524/assets/88a0ceeb1f98bd909ebc6eda2c5232075b6f93d4a158ae1ed1cb4943ab08424c.png)\n\nTwo point masses $A$ and $B$ of mass $12\\,lb$ and $15lb$, respectively, are connected by a rigid, massless rod that is perfectly balanced on a fulcrum, as shown above. If the distance between the fulcrum and $B$ is $4\\,yd,$ find the distance between the fulcrum and $A.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $d$ be the distance between the fulcrum and $A,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14117524/assets/eeaf2a4fbefd3066d28fab6b52cbea58f5adac5cd35887ad012691c9847f5caa.png)\n\nLet's write down the moment of each mass about the fulcrum:\n\n- The moment of mass $A$ equals $-12\\cdot d=-12d\\,\\text{lb}\\cdot \\text{yd}$ (negative since the motion induced by this mass would be counterclockwise).\n\n- The moment of mass $B$ equals $15\\cdot 4=60\\,\\text{lb}\\cdot \\text{yd}$ (positive since the motion induced by this mass would be clockwise).\n\nThe *principle of moments* tells us that for the two masses to perfectly balance, the sum of the moments must equal zero.\n\nTherefore, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60-12d & =0 \\\\ 12d & =60 \\\\ d & =5\\,yd.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
